WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
The CDC figure compares a normal heart with a

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.203
ventricular septal defect.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.287
The ventricular septum normally
separates the two lower chambers.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.140
This institutional artwork is
used for visualization, with no

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.147
CDC endorsement of this study lab.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.408
A ventricular septal defect leaves a communication.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.640
In the supplied postnatal
pattern, higher left ventricular pressure

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.560
sends additional blood toward
the right ventricle and pulmonary

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.384
circulation.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.007
Watch the added stream cross the septum.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.620
A large shunt increases pulmonary flow.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.680
The chapter lists overcirculation
and heart failure among possible

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.363
consequences.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.320
This mechanism differs from
an outflow obstruction or parallel

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:40.870
circuits.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.793
Pediatric heart failure can
cause poor feeding, sweating and

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:45.917
inadequate growth.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:50.520
Nutritional support addresses the
growth problem; medication and corrective

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.746
procedures address their stated targets.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.564
Chapter thirty three, slides
nine, ten and thirty through

00:00:57.564 --> 00:00:59.400
thirty two, support these points.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.044
Shifted released answer labels are marked separately.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.880
Complete murmur mapping and
criteria for later cyanosis remain

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:07.490
source gaps.
